## ABSTRACT
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are attractive for promising applications but plagued by difficult recovery and deployment due to their intrinsic nano/micro powder nature. Although significant efforts have been made to develop separable solid matrixes for MOF supporting, the poor loading stability and durability of MOFs still challenge their engineering applications. Here, we present a  facile  and  effective  approach  to  fabricate  MOF-based  melamine  foams  (MFs)  (denoted  as  MOFiths)  with  ultrahigh  loading stability and operation stability, easy separation, and high-efficient performance for versatile robust applications. By adopting our approach, numbers of typical fragile MOFs characterized with wide ranges of particle size (from \~ nm to $\sim\upmu\mathsf{m}\bar{\mathbf{\upmu}}$ ) can be precisely incorporated  into  MFs  with  controllable  loading  ratios  (up  to  \~ $1,600\%$ ).  Particularly,  the  produced  MOFiths  show  excellent capacities for the highly effective and durable water purifications and acetalization reactions. $100\%$ of organic pollutants can be rapidly destructed within 10 min by MOFiths initiated Fenton or catalytic ozonation processes under five successive cycles while the maximum adsorption capacity of MOFiths toward Pb(II), Cd(II), and $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I I})$ reaches to 422, 222, and $105\mathrm{\mg}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. This study provides a critical solution to substantially facilitate the engineering applications of MOFs for long-term use in practice.
## KEYWORDS
metal-organic framework, melamine foam, ultrastability, versatile applications, long-term use
## Introduction
Worldwide concerned organic pollutants, toxic metals, and even energy-intensive  catalysis  processes  are  calling  for  high-effective multi-functional materials to alleviate their negatively impacts on ecosystems  and  human  health.  Metal-organic  frameworks (MOFs),  constructed  from  organic  linkers  and  metal-oxo nodes  [1, 2],  have  attracted  wide  attention  in  many  important applications,  such  as  water  decontamination  [3–7],  green catalysis  [8–12],  and  gas  storage/separation  [13–15],  because  of their  structural  diversity  and  functional  tunability.  However, owing to the undesirable brittle and fragile nature, MOFs are likely to shatter into tiny particles or fine powders when encountering mechanical destructions or solvent corrosions [16–18]. Moreover, given the insolubility and non-thermoplasticity, MOFs cannot  be  easily  processed  into  separable  monoliths  only  by themselves [19, 20]. This shortage largely increases the net cost of potential  operation  (e.g.,  transportation,  maintenance,  and disposal), thus limiting their engineering applications. Examples of limitations  that  may  cause  serious  problems  include  pipe  clog, catalyst loss, and separation, among which the latter is the origin issue.  Therefore,  the  design  and  creation  of  MOF  composites featuring the property of facile separation and recovery are of great importance.
Significant  efforts  to  fabricate  separable  MOF  composites generally  focused  on  loading  MOF  powders  into  bulk  matrixes (e.g.,  aerogel,  polymers,  textiles,  and  metal  oxide)  by in-situ or post-treatment  methods.  Particularly,  MOF/nanofiber  [21–23], MOF/textile [24–26], MOF/mesh [27, 28], and MOF/ membrane  [29–31]  have  been  developed  with  acceptable separation ability derived from the advantage of these substrates’ physical  architectures.  Unfortunately,  some  of  them  suffer  from complex  fabrication  procedures  while  others  may  lose  the promising  intrinsic  features  of  MOFs  such  as  high  porosity.  In addition,  the  architectures  of  some  substrates  generally  undergo undesirable disintegration when exposing to practical stimulus. As a result, limited MOFs-based products that characterize with great stability and easily separable property are commercially available or employed at present.
In  contrast,  MOF  aerogels  and  foams  constructed  from three-dimensional  (3D)  substrates,  such  as  cellulose/graphene aerogels  [32–34],  and  polyurethane/melamine  foams  [35–37], have hierarchical pores that can improve its mechanical property, solve  separation  problem,  and  benefit  the  overall  mass  transfer regarding pore diffusion. Among these 3D matrixes, the melamine foam (MF) is of great interest due to its high structural stability, good  mechanical  property,  and  easy  accessibility  [38, 39].
However,  the  loaded  MOF  powder  would  be  easily  stripped  off from MF matrix under external force destructions because of the poor  interaction  between  MOFs  and  MF.  Even  the  surface modification  of  MF  still  cannot  fully  overcome  this  big challenge  [35, 40].  Interestingly,  by  designing  a  unique  ball-andstick architecture, Huang et al. developed a series of recyclable and stable  MOF/MF  composites  with  preserved  crystallinity  and hierarchical porosity for high-performance catalysis [17]. But the developed approach requires a sufficient match of MOF size and MF fibrous diameter so that it may be unavailable for producing nanoscaled-MOF/MF  composites.  Considering  the  wide  size distribution (usually from $\sim\mathrm{nm}$ to $\sim\upmu\mathrm{m})$ of different MOFs, a more straightforward, universal, and practical strategy to construct separable  and  ultrastable  MOF/MF  composites  (MOFiths)  is highly desired.
Herein, we report a facile and scalable approach for preparing separable,  ultrastable,  and  multifunctional  MOFiths.  This approach involves two key steps: (i) the preparation of pre-coating slurry.  To  stabilize  the  MOF  powder  during  utilization,  we integrated  poly(vinylidene  fluoride)  (PVDF),  a  typical  cross-link binder,  and  MOF  particles  into  dimethylformamide  (DMF) solution to form the pre-coating slurry. The PVDF was chosen as a  proof  of  concept  because  of  its  strong  refractory  to  most solvents/oxidants/gases  and  porous  architecture  after  thermally induced  phase  separation  (TIPS).  In  addition,  PVDF  produces MOFs  composites  that  retain  the  promising  features  of  MOFs such as high specific surface areas and MOF-based functionality such as active site accessibility and postsynthetic modification [41]; (ii) the dip coating process. The prepared slurry was impregnated into cavities inside MF via simple squeezing, affording ultrastable MOFiths followed by drying at a high temperature via TIPS. This allows  the  direct  use  of  MOFs  with  their  intrinsic  merits  and endows  MOFiths  with  facile  recovery  and  long-term  operation ability  for  engineering  applications.  The  produced  MOFiths display  an  ultrahigh  loading  stability  when  encountering mechanical  destructions  (e.g.,  squeezing,  stirring,  centrifugation, and  ultrasonication)  and  solvent  corrosions.  We  can  also  easily tune the MOF loading ratio (up to $\sim1{,}600\%$ ) within MOFiths by adjusting PVDF/MOFs feeding ratios for fit-for-purpose use. The resulting  MOFiths  show  great  potentials  in  robust  versatile promising  applications,  including  water  purifications  and  green organic synthesis.
## 2    Experimental
## 2.1    Materials
Iron chloride hexahydrate $(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ , copper nitrate trihydrate $\mathrm{(Cu(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3H_{2}O)}$ , zirconium chloride $\mathrm{(ZrCl_{4})}$ , 1,4-benzenedicarb-oxylic  acid $\mathrm{(H_{2}B D C)}$ ,  1,3,5-tricarboxylic  acid $\mathrm{(H_{3}B T C)}$ , acetic acid $(\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O H})$ , polyvinyl pyrrolidone (PVP), DMF, ethanol (EtOH), toluene (TOL), dichloromethane $\mathrm{(CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{)}$ , tetrahydrofuran (THF), methylene blue (MB), $30\ \mathrm{wt}.\%$ hydrogen peroxide $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2})$ , $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{\tt~p~}}$ -nitrophenol  (4-NP),  sodium  thiosulfate pentahydrate $(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{\cdot}5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ ,  hydrochloric  acid  (HCl),  sodium hydroxide $\left(\mathrm{NaOH}\right)$ ,  trisodium  phosphate $\mathrm{(Na_{3}P O_{4})}$ ,  tert-butanol (TBA),  benzaldehyde  (BA),  ethylene  glycol  (EG),  cyclohexane (CH) and mesitylene (internal GC standard) were purchased from Aladdin  Chemical ${\mathrm{Co}}$ .  Ltd.  All  chemicals  were  analytical  grade and  used  without  any  further  purification  if  unspecified.  PVDF powder $(\mathrm{FR904},\sim500,000\ \mathrm{g}.\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ was purchased from Shanghai 3F  New  Materials  Technology  Co.  Ltd.,  China.  Melamine  foam was kindly supported by MY New Energy Technology Co. Ltd., China. Ultrapure water employed in the experiment was obtained from Milli-Q Advantage A10 (Millipore).
## 2.2    Synthesis of MOFs
MIL-53(Fe) was fabricated according to our previous study [42]. Typically, 5 mmol $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and 5 mmol $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ were added into DMF $\mathrm{30~mL},$ ) to form a clear solution at room temperature. Then, the solution was delivered into a reactor equipped with a Teflon liner and kept at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ . After cooling, the solid was obtained  via  centrifugation,  and  successively  washed  with  DMF $(50~\mathrm{mL})$ ), ethanol $(50~\mathrm{mL})$ ) and ultrapure water $(50~\mathrm{mL})$ ) for three times to completely remove impurities. Finally, the brown power was received by vacuum drying $(100^{\circ}\mathrm{C},24\mathrm{h})$ ).
CuBTC was prepared according to a previous work with some modifications [43]. The $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\left(3.4\mathrm{\mmol}\right)$ and $\mathrm{{H_{3}B T C}}$ $\mathrm{(1.8~mmol)}$ was added into ultrapure water $\mathrm{(18~mL)}$ and ethanol $\mathrm{18~mL}$ )  to  form  two  solutions,  respectively.  After  mixing  these two solutions, $20\upmu\mathrm{M}$ of PVP was added to produce the precursor solution.  This  precursor  solution  was  then  transferred  into  an autoclave for the crystallization reaction $120{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ 12 h). The blue solid  was  collected  by  centrifugation,  washed  with  ethanol  and water for 3 times and vacuum dried overnight $(100^{\circ}\mathrm{C},24\mathrm{h})$ .
MIL-88A(Fe)  was  also  fabricated  according  to  our  previous study  [45].  A  precursor  solution  was  prepared  by  adding $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(20\mathrm{\mmol})$ and  fatty  acids  (FA) $(20\mathrm{\mmol})$ into $150\mathrm{mL}$ ultra pure water (UPW) at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ and then moved into an autoclave $(200~\mathrm{mL})$ . The autoclave was kept at $65^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ with oven and left to cool naturally after the reaction. Afterwards, the solid  powder  was  obtained  by  centrifugation,  and  consecutively washed with hot ethanol and UPW to remove residual impurities. Finally, the brown-red product was collected by further drying in a vacuum oven $(100^{\circ}\mathrm{C},24\mathrm{h})$ ).
## 2.3    Synthesis of MOFiths
The  fabrication  procedure  is  illustrated  in Fig. 1(a).  Firstly,  a certain  amount  of  PVDF  powder  was  slowly  added  into  DMF solvent  to  form  a  PVDF/DMF  solution  via  stirring  while  a desirable  amount  of  MOF  powders  were  added  into  another portion  of  DMF  solution  to  form  a  MOF/DMF  suspension  via ultrasonication.  The  PVDF  and  MOF  powder  must  be  added separately  into  individual  DMF  solvent,  or  it  is  impossible  to prepare  the  well  dispersed  and  uniform  MOF  slurry.  Then,  the MOF/DMF  suspension  was  moved  into  PVDF/MOF  solution under stirring to obtain the pre-coating slurry. Afterwards, a piece of MF was immersed and squeezed in the slurry for several times, allowing  for  the  full  diffusion  of  slurry  into  internal  cavities. Finally, the coated MF was subjected to a TIPS procedure ${\mathrm{140~}}^{\circ}{\mathrm{C}},$ $60\ \mathrm{min};$ )  to  obtain  the  MOFith.  The  PVDF/MF  composite  was fabricated  following  the  same  procedure  except  adding  MOF powders. Varying MOFs’ amount and type could receive different MOFiths.  The  loading  amount  and  ratio  of  MIL-53(Fe)  were controlled according to Table S2 in the ESM. All the loading ratio values were presented at the form of mass ratio, where the mass of MF  was  used  as  the  reference.  Each  loading  experiment  was carried out in sextuplet. Other MOFiths were prepared using same procedure  except  changing  MIL-53(Fe)  with  other  MOFs  like Cu-BTC,  UiO-66,  and  MIL-88A(Fe).  The  MIL-53(Fe)/MF composite (denoted as $53(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{MF})$ was also prepared for control by  the  same  procedure  of  MOFiths  except  adding  PVDF.  The PVDF/MF composite was also fabricated for control by the same procedure of MOFiths except adding MIL-53(Fe) powders.
## 2.4 Characterization
X-ray  diffraction  (XRD)  patterns  were  recorded  to  examine  the crystal  structure  of  prepared  MOFs,  MOFiths,  and  other composite samples by a D8 discover X-ray diffractometer (Bruker, Germany) with $\mathrm{Cu}\ K\alpha$ radiation $(\lambda=0.15406\ \mathrm{nm})$ at $2\theta$ ranging from $3^{\mathrm{o}}$ to $30^{\mathrm{o}}$ .  The  molecular  interaction  between  MOFs  and PVDF was revealed by detecting the solid-state nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy of prepared samples on a Bruker Avance NEO spectrometer operating at $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ resonance frequency of $600~\mathrm{MHz}$ using a Bruker double-resonance $4\mathrm{mm}$ magic-angle spinning (MAS) probehead. An ultra 55 field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FE-SEM) (Carl Zeiss, Germany) was used to analyze  the  morphology  of  prepared  samples.  The  elemental mapping was performed by an equipped energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) (X-MaxN, Oxford instrument, $\mathrm{EHT}=10\mathrm{kV},$ . $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms were collected on an ASAP 2020  automated  gas  adsorption  system  (Micromeritics,  USA)  to calculate  surface  areas  and  pore  size  distributions  of  prepared samples. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) data were collected to evaluate  samples’ thermostability  on  a  TGA-50  (SHIMADZU) thermogravimetric  analyzer  with  a  heating  rate  of $10\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ under air atmosphere.
## 2.5    Analysis
The MOF loading stability was analyzed by the mass change of 53(Fe)-MOFiths  after  subjecting  to  mimicking  mechanical destructions and solvent corrosions. In particular, the mimicking mechanical destructions involved squeezing, stirring, centrifugation, and ultrasonication. The squeezing destruction of 53(Fe)-MOFiths was performed at a pressure of about $180~\mathrm{N}$ for 100  times.  The  stirring  destruction  of  53(Fe)-MOFiths  was conducted using an IKA agitator with a constant speed of $300\mathrm{rpm}$ for $60\mathrm{min}$ . The centrifugation destruction of 53(Fe)-MOFiths was performed  at  a  centrifuge  with  a  reactive  centrifugal  force  of $_{10,362g}$ for $60\ \mathrm{min}$ .  The  ultrasonication  destruction  of  53(Fe)- MOFiths  was  conducted  through  putting  it  into  an  ultrasonic apparatus with $240~\mathrm{W}$ power and last for $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ . As for solvent corrosions, a pre-weighted 53(Fe)-MOFith was placed into a vial having certain solvents for one month. Each test was performed in triplicate.  The  mass  change  and  loss  of  53(Fe)-MOFiths  were determined by following equations (Eqs. (1) and (2))
$$
\mathrm{Mass\change}\left(\%\right)=m/m_{0}\times100
$$
$$
\mathrm{Mass}\log(\%)=(1-m/m_{0})\times100
$$
where $m$ and $m_{0}$ represent the weight of 53(Fe)-MOFiths before and  after  mechanical  destructions  or  solvent  corrosions, respectively. Before weighting, 53(Fe)-MOFiths were deposited in the  room  with  constant  temperature $\left(\sim25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\right)$ and  humidity $(65\%)$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ to achieve mass balance with a constant humidity around surrounding environment. The leaching iron after solvent corrosions was measured by using an inductively coupled plasma optical  emission  spectrometer  (ICP-OES,  Agilent  720,  USA). The  MB  concentration  of  withdrawn  sample  in  Fenton-like oxidation  process  was  detected  by  a  ultraviolet–visible  (UV–vis) spectrometer  (Lambda35,  Perkin  Elmer)  at  a  wavelength of $664~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ .  The  4-NP  concentration  was  determined  by  a high  performance  liquid  chromatography  (HPLC),  (Agilent Technologies Series 1260) with a UV–vis detector (Waters 2,489) at $320~\mathrm{nm}$ . The ZORBAX Eclipse XDB-C18 column $3.5\ \mathrm{mm}\ \times$ $4.6~\mathrm{\mm}\ \times\ 150~\mathrm{\mm})$ was  used  as  a  stationary  phase  and maintained at $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . A mixture of $2\mathrm{mM}\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4}$ aqueous solution $(30\%)$ and methanol $(70\%)$ was used as the mobile phase, whose flow  rate  was  set  at $0.75~\mathrm{\mL{\cdot}m i n^{-1}}$ .  A  gas  chromatograph (Shimadzu,  GC-2010ATF,  Japan)  equipped  with  DB-1  capillary column $(30~\mathrm{m}\times0.53~\mathrm{mm}\times1.5~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ with  a  flame  ionization detector was used to analyze the acetalization reaction products. The  total  organic  carbon  (TOC)  of  withdraw  samples  was estimated by a TOC analyzer (Elementar, LiquiTOCⅡ).
## 2.6    Evaluation of  the  catalytic  and  adsorption performances
As  a  concept  of  proof, $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}$ was  selected  for multi-performance investigations. The Fenton-like performance of $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}$ was  evaluated  by  activating $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ for  MB destruction at a pH value of 4.5. Typically, a cuboid piece ${\mathrm{(4~cm~\times~}}$ $2\ \mathrm{cm}\ \times1\ \mathrm{cm},$ )  of $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}$ was  added  into $20~\mathrm{mL}$ MB solution $(50\ \mathrm{mg\cdotL^{-1}})$ )  and  squeezed  to  ensure  the  full  contact  of catalyst and pollutant. Then, $20\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was added to initiate the Fenton-like oxidation process. The quenching experiment was conducted  by  adding $4\ \mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ TBA  into  the  oxidation  system. $0.5~\mathrm{mL}$ of  sample  was  withdrawn  at  given  time  intervals  and filtered  by $0.45\quad{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ membrane  for  MB  concentration quantification. After easy separation by a tweezer, the catalyst was renewed  by  washing  with  ethanol  and  vacuum  drying $(100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ overnight. Each Fenton-like oxidation experiment was carried out in triplicate.
The  catalytic  ozonation  performance  of $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}$ was evaluated  by  4-NP  removal  in  water  at  a $\mathrm{\pH}$ value  of  7.0.  In  a typical experiment, ten pieces of cylindrical $r=2\ {\mathrm{cm}}.$ , $h=1\mathrm{cm})$ $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}s$ were  settled  into  a  designed  column  with $300~\mathrm{mL}$ 4-NP solution $(100\mathrm{mg{\cdot}L^{-1}})$ . $25\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ of ozone, produced from an ozone generator (Tonglin Technology 3S-A, China) using high purity $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ as input gas, was bubbled into the bottle of reactor through  an  aerator.  At  given  time  intervals,  about $5.0~\mathrm{mL}$ of sample was withdrawn and placed into a centrifuge tube for 4-NP determination  after  it  was  filtered  using  a $0.45~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ microfilter. After sampling, the residual reactive oxidation species (ROS) and $\mathrm{O}_{3}$ were immediately eliminated by 0.1 M ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{S}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ solution. The renew  procedure  was  identical  to  the  Fenton-like  oxidation experiment.
The  adsorption  performance  of $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}$ was  studied using ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{II}})$ , Cd(II), and $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ as target metals in the adsorption tests. Desired solutions of $\operatorname{Pb}(\operatorname{II})$ , $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ , and $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ were prepared by  diluting  the  stock  solution $(1,000~\mathrm{\mg{\cdot}L^{-1}})$ to  a  certain concentration $(50{-}800~\mathrm{mg{\cdot}L^{-1}}),$ . The $\mathrm{\pH}$ value of prepared solution was adjusted to 5.0 with 0.1 M HCl. A piece of $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}$ was  added  to $40~\mathrm{mL}$ of  metal  solutions  to  form  an  operating mixture  for  the  adsorption  tests.  The  concentrations  of  heavy metal  ions  were  determined  by  an  ICP-OES  (Agilent  720).  The adsorption capacity $\mathrm{\prime}Q_{\mathrm{e}},$ Eq. (3)) was used to assess the adsorption performance of $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}$
$$
Q_{\mathrm{e}}=\left(C_{\mathrm{0}}-C_{\mathrm{e}}\right)/M\times V
$$
where $C_{0}\left(\mathrm{mg\cdotL^{-1}}\right)$ and $C_{\mathrm{e}}\left(\mathrm{mg\cdotL^{-1}}\right)$ are the initial concentration and equilibrium concentration, respectively. $V\left(\mathrm{mL}\right)$ is the volume of operating suspension. $M\left({\mathrm{mg}}\right)$ represents the mass of loaded MIL53(Fe).  For  the  kinetic  adsorption  experiments,  the  initial concentration was set at $100\ \mathrm{mg{L}^{-1}}$ . The adsorption capacities at time  internals  were  monitored  and  analyzed.  To  obtain  the adsorption isotherms, different concentrations (50, 100, 200, 400, and $800\ \mathrm{mg{\cdot}L^{-1}},$ ) of metal solutions were prepared and incubated for $180\ \mathrm{min}$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ .  As  for  statistical  analysis,  the  result  is shown  as  a  mean  of  3  replicates.  Pseudo-first-order  and pseudo-second-order  models  were  used  to  fit  the  adsorption kinetic  data.  The  isotherms  were  fitted  by  Langmuir  and Freundlich  models.  Furthermore,  experimental  results  from adsorption  tests  were  used  to  determine  the  distribution coefficient $(K_{\mathrm{d}})$ for  metal  uptake  using  the  following  equation (Eq. (4))
$$
K_{\mathrm{d}}=\left(C_{\mathrm{i}}-C_{\mathrm{e}}\right)/C_{\mathrm{e}}\times\left(V/m\right)
$$
where $C_{\mathrm{i}}$ is  the  initial  metal  ion  concentration, $C_{\mathrm{e}}$ is  the  final equilibrium metal ion concentration, $V\left(\mathrm{mL}\right)$ is the volume of the treated solution and $m\left({\bf g}\right)$ is the mass of MOF used. In adsorption experiment, the $V/m$ ratio was set as $\sim1{,}267\mathrm{mL}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
The catalytic performance of $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}$ for benzaldehyde  acetalization  was  tested  according  to  following procedures. Firstly, 1 mmol of benzaldehyde, 18 of mmol ethylene glycol, $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of cyclohexane, and $70\mathrm{mg}$ of mesitylene were added into  a  round-bottom  flask  filling  with  5  pieces  of  fanshaped $(r=2\mathrm{cm},h=1\mathrm{cm},\theta=45^{\circ})$ $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}s$ . Then, the reactor was  transferred  into  an  oil  bath  to  initiate  the  reaction  at  a temperature of $90^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . The reaction was implemented under reflux and  stirring  for  a  certain  time.  After  reaction,  the  product  was analyzed by a GC method. The regeneration of $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}s$ was identical to the Fenton-like oxidation experiment.
## 3    Results and discussion
## 3.1    PVDF  dosage  optimization  for  facile  MOFith synthesis
## 3.2    MOF loading stability of MOFiths
We employed nanoscale MIL-53(Fe) $\sim500\mathrm{nm})$ , a widely applied iron-based  MOF  in  the  water  purification  and  organic synthesis [46–48], as a model to screen the preparation condition of MOFiths. The element mapping of MOFiths first identified the good incorporation of MIL-53(Fe), PVDF, and MF (Fig. S4 in the ESM). Then, we fixed the concentration of PVDF/DMF solution at $20\ \mathrm{mg\cdotmL^{-1}}$ for  investigating  the  MOF  loading  stability  and limitation.  Homogeneous  suspensions  were  formed  after  adding different  amounts  of  MIL-53(Fe)/DMF  suspension  into  the PVDF/DMF  solution  (Fig. S5(a)  in  the  ESM).  After  loading MIL-53(Fe),  the  white  MF  was  uniformly  turned  to  yellow MOFith, referred to 53(Fe)-MOFith (Fig. S5(b) in the ESM). We could also easily design the shape of 53(Fe)-MOFith by tailoring the  original  geometry  of  used  MF.  Importantly,  negligible  mass loss  of  53(Fe)-MOFith  was  observed  when  encountering  strong external  mechanical  destructions,  such  as  squeezing  (Fig. 1(b), Movie ESM1, and Fig. S6(a) in the ESM), stirring (Fig. 1(b) and Fig. S5(c) in the ESM), centrifugation (Fig. 1(b) and Fig. S5(c) in the  ESM),  and  ultrasonication  (Fig. 1(b) and Fig. S5(c)  in  the ESM).  Whereas,  the  53(Fe)/MF  counterpart  (fabricated  without adding PVDF) suffered from a serious loss of MOFs even under squeezing  (Movie  ESM2  and Fig. S6(a)  in  the  ESM).  Moreover, the  53(Fe)-MOFith  could  stay  stable  in  most  solvents  for  longterm operation (i.e., 30 days) with little powder $(<4\%)$ (Fig. S6(b) in the ESM) or iron leaching $(<2\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1})$ (Fig. S6(c) in the ESM). Therefore, ultrastable 53(Fe)-MOFith was successfully achieved by using  PVDF  as  a  functional  binding  agent,  which  could  be ascribed  to  the  significant  hydrogen  bond  interaction  between fluorine of PVDF and the terminal OH in the MIL-53(Fe) surface $(\mathrm{F_{PVDF}}{\cdots}\mathrm{HO}_{\mathrm{MIL}\cdot53(\mathrm{Fe})})$ [39, 49].
## 3.3    MOF loading ratio regulation on MOFiths
High  MOF  loading  content/ratio  is  particularly  desired  for advanced  functional  MOF  composites.  We  investigated  the loading  limitation  of  our  approach  by  varying  the  MOF  dosage (20, 40, 80, 120, and $200~\mathrm{{mg}{\cdot m L^{-1}}}$ ) (Table S2 in the ESM) in the preparation formula. The corresponding MOFiths were denoted as $53(\mathrm{Fe)\mathrm{-}M O F i t h_{20}},$ $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{40}.$ , $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{80},$ $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{120},$ and $53(\mathrm{Fe)\mathrm{-MOFith}_{200},}$ respectively.  Surprisingly,  this  strategy  could  obtain  the  highest  MOF  loading  ratio $(\sim1,600\%)$ among  previous  studies  (Fig. 2(a)).  In  contrast,  the unstable $53(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{MF}$ composite only got a maximum loading ratio of $36\%$ . Notably, a strong linear relationship was clearly observed between the MOF dosage and the average loading ratio (Fig. 2(b)). To  this  end,  we  could  easily  tune  the  loading  ratio  of  MOFs ranging  from $\sim170\%$ to $\sim1,600\%$ by  adjusting  their  feeding dosage.  All  these  53(Fe)-MOFiths  with  distinctive  MOF  ratios have  ultrahigh  loading  stability  under  squeezing  (Fig. 2(c)).  The FE-SEM  images  of  the  cross-section  of $53(\mathrm{Fe})/\mathrm{MF}$ composite shows that the MIL-53(Fe) particles regularly laid on the surface of the  MF  filament  (Fig. S7  in  the  ESM).  Unlike  the  53(Fe)/ MF  composite,  53(Fe)-MOFiths  exhibits  a  morphology  of cage-locked-cluster through the filament. Such unique morphology  delivers  an  opportunity  for  the  MF  to  hold  a  high content of MOFs with ultrastability. More importantly, the cluster gradually becomes larger with increasing the MOF dosage in the FE-SEM images of the cross-section of 53(Fe)-MOFiths (Fig. S7 in the  ESM),  verifying  the  full  diffusion  of  slurry  into  MF  internal cavities  and  suggesting  the  increase  of  MOF  loading  ratio. Notably,  several  other  MOFs (Fig. 3)  with  a  diversity  of  particle sizes  ranging  from  nanoscales  to  microscales  have  been successfully  processed  into  MOF-foams  (e.g.,  88A(Fe)-MOFith, CuBTC-MOFith,  and  UiO(66)-MOFith)  (Fig. S8  in  the  ESM) using  this  approach,  demonstrating  the  high  generality  and applicability of the proposed novel strategy.
3.4    Insights  into  the  impacts  of  loading  ratio  on MOFiths’ structure at multi levels
## 3.5    Ultrastable  and  separable  MOFiths  for  versatile environmental applications
Water  scarcity  and  the  high  risk  of  toxic  organic/inorganic contaminants  to  water  supply  have  stimulated  environmental scientists/engineers to develop advanced materials with engineering  potentials  for  sustainable  purposes  [52–54].  As  our MOFiths  are  expected  to  be  ideal  candidates  possessing  easy separation  and  ultrastable  features,  we  first  investigates  the catalytic performance of $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}$ via advanced oxidation processes (AOPs) based technology using $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (i.e., Fenton-like oxidation) or $\mathrm{O}_{3}$ (i.e., catalytic ozonation) as the oxidants. This is mainly  because  Lewis  acid  sites  within  MIL-53(Fe)  can catalytically  transfer $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}/\mathrm{O}_{3}$ into  highly  ROS  for  the  efficient destruction  of  organic  pollutants  [42, 45].  We  choose  MB  and 4-NP, two refractory organic compounds to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{O}_{3},$ as the model substrates for Fenton-like oxidation and catalytic ozonation over $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20},$ respectively.
Figure  5(a) displays  the  degradation  profile  of  MB  by Fenton-like  oxidation  using $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}$ .  About $50\%$ MB could  adsorb  onto $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}$ because  of  its  hierarchical pore  structure  and  the  abundant  active  adsorption  sites  of $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}$ . No MB destruction is observed by individually adding $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ or  MF  while  the  degradation  of  MB  is  quickly completed  within $8\ \mathrm{min}$ in  the  Fenton-like  oxidation  system $\left(53(\mathrm{Fe)\mathrm{-}M O F i t h}_{20}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)$ owing to the presence of coordinatively unsaturated  iron  centers  (Fig. 4(d)),  which  could  serve  as  active site for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ capturing and activation through the Lewis acid-base interaction  [55, 56].  The  excellent  catalytic  performance  over Fenton-like oxidation of $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}$ could be ascribed to the effective  mass  transfer  and  easily  accessible  active  sites.  The adsorbed MB molecules on the surface of $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}$ could be  easily  degraded  by  the in-situ generation  of  ROS  via $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ decomposition.  Strong  suppression  of  TBA  on  MB  degradation suggests  the  active  hydroxyl  radical  is  responsible  for  MB degradation. Unlike the powder catalyst, we easily separate 53(Fe)- $\mathrm{MOFith}_{20}$ by a tweezer for cycling purposes as a proof of concept (Fig. S10  in  the  ESM).  Little  decay $(<1.0\%)$ of  MB  degradation rate  is  occurred  during  the  successive  five  runs  (Fig. 5(b)), identifying the good recycle performance of $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}$ .
For the catalytic ozonation process, we design a reactor using ten pieces of cylindrical $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}s$ as fillers to effectively decompose $\mathrm{~O}_{3}.$ The ozone bubble from the bottom aeration passes through the hierarchical pores and contacts with abundant Lewis acid  sites  within $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}$ to  generate  ROS  [57]. Therefore,  4-NP  could  be  completely  removed  with  a  high pseudo-first-order  kinetic  rate  of $0.345\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ (Fig. 5(c)). $85.2\%$ removal  of  TOC  is  achieved  at $30\ \mathrm{min}$ .  Noting  that  MF  itself shows little impact on the 4-NP destruction as no increasement or decrease  in  removal  efficiency  is  found  when  comparing  with ozonation alone system. More importantly, after easy recovery, the renewed $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}$ still have a high TOC removal $(80.2\%)$ in the $6^{\mathrm{{th}}}$ run with $100\%$ degradation rate (Fig. 5(d)).
To clarify the effect of ROS on MOFiths’ structure, we perform mechanical  tests  to  investigate  the  elasticity  change  of  MF  and MOFiths  before  and  after  Fenton-like  reaction  as  a  proof  of concept.  Results  (Fig. S11(a)  in  the  ESM)  turn  out  that  little change  could  be  observed  in  the  stress-strain  curves  and  elastic modulus, indicating the robust ROS resistance nature of MF and MOFiths.  In  addition,  the  spend $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}$ also  exhibites good stability after soaping in the water, EtOH, and TOL solutions for one month as its mass loss and iron leakage remain the same with the originally unused counterpart (Fig. S11(b) in the ESM). These  observations  highlight  the  practicability  of  developed MOFiths for long-term water treatment applications.
Moreover, $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}$ also  presents  effective  toxic metal  removal  capability  (i.e., $\mathrm{Pb(II)}$ ,  Cd(II),  and $\mathrm{Cu(II)}),$ These  adsorption  processes  are  well  described  by  the pseudosecond-order  kinetic  model  (Fig. 6(a))  and  Langmuir  isotherm (Fig. 6(b)),  identifying  the  chemisorption  nature  [58, 59].  The maximum  adsorption  capacity  of $\mathrm{Pb(II)}$ , $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ ,  and $\mathrm{Cu(II)}$ reached to 422, 222, and $105~\mathrm{mg{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ , respectively (Table S5 in the ESM).  According  to  the  Refs  [5, 60, 61],  free $-{\mathrm{COOH}}$ groups remain in organic linkers of MIL-53(Fe) could act as effective sites for  these  toxic  metal  adsorptions  via  complexation  interaction. Notably,  the ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{II}})$ and $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ distribution  coefficients $\left(K_{\mathrm{d}}\right)$ (Fig. 6(c))  for $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}$ are  solely  higher  than  threshold values  of  5,000  and  104  at  low  initial concentrations  [37, 62], matching or even exceeding those of state-of-the-art MOF-based adsorbents  [63–65].  Moreover,  no  evident  loss  of  adsorption capacity and MOFith mass is observed after 5 cycles (Fig. 6(d)). These  results  indicate  that  our  designed  MOFiths  could  be potential  candidates  for  durable  MOFs-based  AOPs  and  metal capture without separation dilemma.
In addition to water purifications, MOFs catalyzes fine chemical synthesis  also  suffers  from  similarly  problematic  separation  and energy-intensive  process  in  some  cases,  which  ultimately  poses negative  impacts  on  the  environment.  We further  screen  the catalysis and recycling performance of $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}$ over the acetalization of aldehyde, which is an important reaction toward chemical  transformations  in  organic  synthesis  but  needs  green promotions [66–68]. As shows in Table S6 in the ESM, we treat
BA  with  EG  in  cyclohexane  with  or  without  the  catalyst.  By comparison  to  control  groups  in  entry $1{-}4,\ 53(\mathrm{Fe){-}M O F i t h}_{20}$ (entry 6) have an excellent catalytic yield $(93\%)$ of benzaldehyde glycol  acetals  after $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ reaction.  The  yield  almost  keeps unchanged $(67\%$ , entry 7) when separating $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}$ from the  reaction  solution  at $6\mathrm{~h~}$ .  Meanwhile,  a  similar  yield  of $64\%$ (entry  5)  is  also  observed  in  the  presence  of $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}$ . These results indicate that the loaded MIL-53(Fe) within 53(Fe)- $\mathrm{\Delta\mathrm{MOFith}_{20}}$ is  responsible  for  the  efficient  catalysis.  Moreover,  the yield drops from $93\%$ to $16\%$ when adding pyridine, suggesting the  Lewis  acid  sites  in $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}$ are  the  active  sites  for such  acetalization  [69].  Remarkably, $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}$ displays considerable successive recycle performance, which is ascribed to its  high  chemical  and  mechanical  stability.  Of  note,  the  original catalytic  efficiency  of $53(\mathrm{Fe)-MOFith}_{20}$ does  not  suffer  from  a significant loss at $10^{\mathrm{th}}$ recycling (Fig. S12 in the ESM), highlighting its greater potential in catalytic applications than powder MOFs. Taking  both  structure  and  property  into  account,  as-developed MOFiths  with  ultrahigh  stability  and  separation  performance presented  greater  mass  transfer  capacity  and  active  site accessibility  for  versatile  applications  when  comparing  with conventional materials such as metal oxide.
## 4    Conclusions
In  summary,  we  developed  a  facile  and  practical  strategy  to engineer  nanoscale  and  microscale  MOFs  into  multifunctional monoliths  with  ultrahigh  loading  stability  and  easy  separation property for versatile promising applications. By this method, we could  tailor  the  formula  to  produce  the  highest  loading  ratio $(\sim\ 1,600\%)$ of  MOFs  among  previous  reports.  Our  MOFiths exhibited  excellent  loading  stability  when  encountering  various mechanical  destructions  and  solvent  corrosions.  Owing  to  the robust  structure  and  ultrahigh  loading  stability  of  MOFs,  the efficient  and  durable  AOPs,  heavy  metal  removal,  and acetalization of aldehyde could be easily achieved. Our approach might open up new avenues for the rational design of scalable and multifunctional MOF composites and the engineering applications  of  MOFs-based  materials  from  the  nanoscale  to microscale.
